    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2022-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">1 ETS for Namibia developed</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1: Design Key Building Blocks for a National Carbon Emissions Trading Scheme (ETS) that can also be applicable regionally:
1. Report focusing on Namibia&#x2019;s crediting framework Capacity Gaps and Support Needs was produced as catalyst for an ETS framework which is currently under development based on desk review conducted. This will also result in the development of a Draft Emission Reduction Regulation under the Environmental Management Act of 2007 of Namibia 
2. Sectoral capacity-building on the training, guidelines, operating manuals, and advocacy materials continue to be developed.

Output 2: Technical and Institutional capacity building for MEFT and relevant institutions including private sector:
1. Sectoral training materials including the training and advocacy materials, Carbon Market design and operation manuals are currently under development, with training and capacity-building workshops: February 2023. 
2. UNDP Namibia prepared mission TORs shared with the local Japanese embassy and JICA in which the government is exploring ways to incorporate Japanese companies to take part in and benefit from the promotion of carbon markets in Namibia. A Namibian delegation aims to undertake a study tour to Japan in March 2023 to learn from the Japanese experience on Carbon Markets, and also examine Namibia&#x2019;s readiness for carbon trading.  
3. An additional knowledge exchange mission TORs were developed for study tour to be undertaken with UNDP Ghana in February 2023.
4. A roadmap is being drafted based on the best practices and lessons learned from various fora and the carbon market framework.

Output 3:  Enabling Investment Climate and Operationalization Planning for Market Mechanism in Namibia:

1.Digital systems are an important technological element of the Paris Climate Agreement to enable transparency and credibility to the NDC reporting, as well as to enable countries to trade mitigation outcomes effectively in order to secure finance for climate change mitigation as per Article 6. As such, Namibia participated in and shared its experiences on the first virtual meeting with the Community of Practice Keeping Track - Digital Public Goods for the Paris Agreement . Namibia provided input on setting up a digital system for part of NDC MRV system.

2. The project has been well received by various stakeholders; it was also presented at the UNFCCC COP 27 at various side events to mobilize additional technical and financial resources. This resulted in the World Bank pledging additional funding to the government of Namibia to further strengthen the project.</narrative>
      </comment>
